This study developed an integrated control-to-fatigue assessment framework for a flywheel energy storage system supported by active magnetic bearings. The framework links the PMSM torque command, flywheel-speed response, speed-dependent AMB dynamics, nominal centrifugal-stress history, rainflow cycle counting, and Palmgren–Miner cumulative damage. Six MPC weighting combinations were investigated and compared with a baseline controller to quantify the trade-off among speed tracking, torque smoothness, AMB actuation demand, rotor motion, power consumption, and fatigue-equivalent life. The results show that the controller weights have a substantial influence on both transient performance and long-term fatigue indicators. Setting 1 provided the most balanced overall response for the operating profile investigated. It reduced the speed overshoot from \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
